\subsection{Method \code{compute_steepest_ascent()}}{
Compute the path of steepest ascent (or descent) from a
fitted first-order model. Returns a sequence of operating conditions
along the gradient direction, along with the predicted response at
each step. This is the standard follow-up procedure when a screening
design identifies significant main effects and the goal is to move
toward the optimum before running a second-order (RSM) design.
\subsection{Usage}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}\preformatted{DoeAnalyzer$compute_steepest_ascent(
  model,
  factors,
  step_size = 1,
  n_steps = 5,
  maximize = TRUE
)}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Arguments}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
\describe{
\item{\code{model}}{A fitted \code{lm} object containing a first-order model
(intercept + main effects only; interactions and quadratic terms
are ignored when computing the gradient).}

\item{\code{factors}}{List of factor definitions (as stored on an
\code{IqrDoePlan} object), where each element has \verb{$name} and \verb{$levels}.
The factor levels are used to convert coded steps back to actual
engineering units.}

\item{\code{step_size}}{Numeric scalar. The step size in coded units for the
factor with the largest absolute coefficient. Default 1.0 (one
coded unit per step for the reference factor).}

\item{\code{n_steps}}{Integer scalar. Number of points to generate along the
path. Default 5.}

\item{\code{maximize}}{Logical. If \code{TRUE} (default), compute the path of
steepest ascent (increase the response). If \code{FALSE}, compute the
path of steepest descent (decrease the response).}
}
\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}
\subsection{Returns}{
A data frame with one row per step. Columns include the
coded and actual factor values, the step number, and the predicted
response. An attribute \code{"gradient"} holds the normalized gradient
vector.
}
}
